  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\n\nWe introduce a fundamentally different optimization strategy based on multi-stage refinement. This approach combines three phases: coarse optimization using a population of parameterized functions, fine-grained refinement via iterative dimensionality reduction, and advanced global search using differential evolution. The key innovation is leveraging analytical parameterization (frequency domain) instead of direct function search and introducing adaptive dimensionality reduction to balance exploration and exploitation. This addresses the optimization landscape challenges by working in transformed spaces and progressively refining dimensions of freedom.\n\nAs opposed to previous methods that treated the step function directly in the spatial domain, we:\n\n1. In the coarse phase, represent functions in frequency space (Fourier series) parameterized by amplitude coefficients\n2. In the refinement phase, rotate the optimization landscape by applying frequency domain transformations\n3. During the final phase, use dimensionality reduction to systematically minimize while maintaining constraints\n\nThese changes allow escaping local minima that typically trap spatial-domain optimization on this highly non-convex problem.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n\nWe model the step function h(x) \u2208 [0,1] on [0,2] with integral 1. The optimization problem is:\n\nMinimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nRepresentation:\n- Fourier series: h(x) \u2248 \u03a3 C\u2096 \u03c9\u2096(x), where \u03c9\u2096(x) = cos(2\u03c0k x / T) for k=0,1,... with T=2\n- Constraints: sum\u2096 |C\u2096|\u00b2 \u2264 1 (energy constraint), transformed to integral = 1 using Parseval's theorem\n\nExact Objective:\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = 1/2 * (2 - cross-corr(h,h)[k]/(n dx)) with n=250\n\nKey Variables:\n- Frequency coefficients {C\u2096} with C\u2080 defined as height adjustment\n- Effective degrees of freedom controlled by max_freq\n\nAdaptive dimensionality reduction:\n- Parametrize energy distribution (spectral radius) across frequencies\n- Maintain feasibility via Cholesky decomposition reconstruction\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n\n1. Global constants:\n   - TARGET_SUM = n/2 (integral constraint)\n   - n_points = 250\n   - dx = 2.0/n_points\n\n2. Phase 1: Coarse frequency space optimization (500 function evaluations)\n   - Represent h(x) via 15 frequency coefficients from k=0 to 14\n   - Objective = max\u2693 [max_k cross-correlation term] * dx\n   - Variables: C\u2081\u2096 for k=1..14, with C\u2080 determined by constraints\n   - Constraints: sum |C\u2096|\u00b2 \u2264 (2/\u03c0)\u00b2ln(1/target_sum)?\n   More precisely enforce \u222bh=1 by projecting reconstructed function to target\n\n3. Phase 2: Cyclic Refinement Algorithm (CR)\n   - Areduce effective degrees of freedom from max_freq=14 down to min_freq=0\n   - At each stage, select subset of {C\u2081,..,C_{f}} to optimize while fixing lower indices\n   - Objective: minimize max correlation between {C\u2081..C_f} and {C\u2081..C_{f-k}}\n\n4. Phase 3: Differential Evolution (DE)\n   - Optimizer: DE/rand/1/bin with mutation factor F=0.9, crossover Cr=0.9\n   - Population: 200 parameter vectors {C} in the finally reduced space\n   - Fitness: directly the adversarial correlation value\n\n5. Phase 4: Local refinement with projected gradient updates\n   - Requires Lapack's dsyev for eigenvalue decomposition\n   - (useful for very fine optimization after global search)\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n\n- Base: 14-dimensional optimization in frequency space\n- Crossover: parameterized False for linear dimensionality reduction\n- DE variants: can swap between DE/rand/1, DE/best/1 or JADE\n- Dimension: default 14, can search 10 or 15 with competition between phases\n\n6. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n\nRunning time: tailormade per phase duration:\n- Phase1: 400 seconds\n- Phase2: 600 seconds (scaling with frequency range)\n- Phase3: 500 seconds\n- Phase4: 100 seconds\n\nTotal time: estimates 1600 seconds per run.\n\nImplementation uses:\n- SciPy for DE and basic calculus\n- Numba for objective function compilation (note: need to install numba)\n- Parallel processing of DE population evaluation\n- Feasibility enforced by Cholesky reconstruction + projection\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n\n1. Check: np.min(h) >= -small, np.max(h) <=1+small, np.mean(h)*dx \u2248 1\n2. Re-evaluate final candidate for C5\n3. Sanit checks: oscillation test, phase-space volume test\n\nReturn 3-plet: (h_values, C5_value, n_points)\n\nAdaptive layering ensures feasible solutions throughout, making sharp objective corridors rather than isolated minima.\n\nNote: frequency space optimization allows convexification of many terms due to Parseval's theorem and allows efficient usage of gradient information when present.",
